WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
Why can doubling an enzyme's substrate produce far less than double the product?

2
00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:11.260
Watch this enzyme make 45 micromoles per minute at 12 mM substrate.

3
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.720
What happens at 24

4
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:26.433
In its simplest form, picture each enzyme as a toll booth with no waiting lane.

5
00:00:27.653 --> 00:00:30.433
A substrate car enters only an empty booth.

6
00:00:31.673 --> 00:00:35.453
A busy booth converts that car to product, then opens again.

7
00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:39.557
With little substrate, most booths stand empty.

8
00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:45.857
Nearly every extra car finds one, so adding substrate almost proportionally

9
00:00:45.857 --> 00:00:47.397
increases product flow.

10
00:00:48.657 --> 00:00:52.017
The limiting factor is arrivals, not the booths' working speed.

11
00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:56.297
Add more substrate, and empty booths become scarce.

12
00:00:57.877 --> 00:01:00.737
Cars finding busy booths drive on and try again later.

13
00:01:02.277 --> 00:01:05.897
Product still flows, but each added car buys less extra speed.

14
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:08.607
Here is the guiding idea.

15
00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:15.687
Velocity equals kcat, one busy enzyme's turnover pace, times the amount of busy

16
00:01:15.687 --> 00:01:21.887
enzyme. Substrate changes the busy amount, but it cannot make each busy enzyme work

17
00:01:21.887 --> 00:01:22.887
faster.

18
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:26.077
Now transform the toll plaza into a graph.

19
00:01:27.437 --> 00:01:32.397
Substrate concentration runs across the bottom; initial velocity climbs upward.

20
00:01:33.657 --> 00:01:38.417
The moving car counter becomes points on the Michaelis-Menten curve, computed from

21
00:01:38.417 --> 00:01:39.417
the equation.

22
00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:46.657
Velocity equals Vmax times substrate concentration, divided by Km plus substrate

23
00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:51.477
concentration. The fraction is the share of enzyme that is busy.

24
00:01:52.757 --> 00:01:56.097
As that share approaches one, velocity approaches Vmax.

25
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:02.217
Km marks the substrate concentration where that busy fraction is one half.

26
00:02:03.557 --> 00:02:06.197
Therefore, velocity is half of Vmax there.

27
00:02:07.617 --> 00:02:12.137
Km is a half-speed point, not automatically a measure of binding affinity.

28
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:17.350
Vmax is the speed approached when nearly every enzyme is busy.

29
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.610
It equals kcat times total enzyme concentration.

30
00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:26.770
More substrate cannot raise that ceiling, but adding enzyme can.

31
00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:34.073
Return to our enzyme: Vmax is 60 micromoles per minute, and Km is 4 mM.

32
00:02:35.373 --> 00:02:38.873
At 12 mM, three quarters are busy, giving 45.

33
00:02:40.153 --> 00:02:44.153
At 24 about six sevenths are busy, giving roughly 51.

34
00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:51.503
Doubling substrate raised velocity only about 14 percent, because just one quarter

35
00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:53.763
of the enzyme was empty initially.

36
00:02:55.223 --> 00:02:59.143
Far below Km, doubling substrate nearly doubles velocity.

37
00:03:00.463 --> 00:03:02.503
Near saturation, it barely helps.

38
00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:08.447
So ask how much enzyme is still empty before predicting a substrate effect.

39
00:03:09.627 --> 00:03:14.407
The curve bends because the busy fraction approaches one, not because catalysis

40
00:03:14.407 --> 00:03:19.747
stops. More substrate adds speed only by filling empty enzymes.
